| P1 (round 2) — the transcription deadline path cannot preserve the required successful capture | blocker | P11 (decision 1, P21 step 4, provider-convention caller table) | **The stored capture always wins.** Once `attachments.put` has returned the capture is complete (R3): the module authors no interruption record after that point and never releases the segment itself. The transcription request is bounded by `min(timeout_seconds, transcription_window)` with `transcription_window = (expiry − now) − 1 s` — R3's own `skipped:deadline` reserve, which refuses to *start* a request in the last second and, by the same constant, closes a *running* one — so the record is always `success` with its `audio_ref`, stamped at the module's own decision instant on the clock — at the window edge `expiry − 1 s` when the bound fires on time, later but still `< expires_at` when the wake is delayed by less than the reserve (round 3 V1, decision 1) — and adopted by the phase 1 in-time rule (`test_a_confirmation_landing_in_time_survives_a_late_adoption`). R10 is not involved on this path: its late adoption governs the recorder half (P10, `capture_timed_out`) and could not help here — a late `success` is never adopted, and a `timeout`/`error` record would lose the capture R3 requires. The three triggers that abandon a pending request (its bound, `drain()`, a `CancelledError` after storing) all end `success` + `transcription_status: "failed:stt_timed_out"`; only a call still recording reaches `expiry`. The 1 s never moves the recorder's kill, which stays at `expiry` (AC39); the amended AC41 authorizes this window as the second of exactly two deadline subtractions (round 3 V4), and P21 step 4 checks that no third exists. Tested through the real executor at `t = 98.99` / `99.0` / adoption parked to `100.5` / a wake delayed to `99.6` (AC42), with the attachment still leased and `COUNTER_ACTION_TIMEOUTS == 0`. |
